    "body": "# Configuration Mode\n\nAdds a configuration mode to Hazel for editing editor settings as Hazel programs, with two slides: **Colors** (adds dark, high-contrast, and high-contrast dark schemes) and **Shortcuts** (rebinds the command-palette actions).\n\n<img width=\"946\" height=\"585\" alt=\"Screenshot From 2025-10-14 11-54-50\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/777ee1f4-6369-430f-aac0-48d56c08c96b\" />\n\n> Screenshot predates the seeds/palette/roles rework and the colour picker \u2014 it shows the mode, not the current slide.\n\n## How\n\n`ConfigurationMode.re` is split out of `ScratchMode.re`. Each slide is a Hazel program analyzed against a type, read back after evaluation, and applied to the running editor.\n\n**Colors** \u2014 `hazel-programs/config/colors.hz` is committed Hazel text, embedded at build time with `ppx_blob` the way doc slides are. It layers *seeds* \u2192 *palette* \u2192 *roles*: ~38 seeds feed a palette, and `style/roles.css` exposes the semantic roles the stylesheets consume. Every `style/*.css` file moved off palette names onto roles, with `make lint-css` (`scripts/lint_css_roles.py`) as the ratchet. `dark_mode` and `high_contrast` are `^^check` flags in the program and they compose: a single `case` on the pair at the bottom selects one of four schemes (light, dark, high-contrast light, high-contrast dark), reading each flag exactly once, so adding a scheme costs one line rather than one conditional per colour. Each scheme carries its own seeds \u2014 dark is *not* derivable from light, as most seeds are re-hued. `BuiltinsColor`/`BuiltinsADT`/`BuiltinsBase` add a `ColorValue` type and the colour arithmetic the program needs; `ColorProj.re` puts a direct-manipulation OKLCH picker (with an RGB tab) on every colour literal.\n\n**Shortcuts** \u2014 `ShortcutAction.re` is a single registry every action flows through, so the slide, the command palette, and the key handling cannot disagree. The slide term is generated from that registry and analyzed against its action set; `KeybindingProj.re` records a binding on click \u2192 chord \u2192 Escape. Resolved bindings land in `Settings.Model.shortcut_overrides` and are applied when the palette is built.\n\n## Testing\n\n`test/ConfigSlideCheck.re` is a shared harness pinning that each slide typechecks against its analyzed type, that the analysis is engaged, and that Colors still evaluates. Plus `Test_Color`, `Test_ColorConfiguration`, `Test_ConfigurationMode`, `Test_ShortcutAction`, `Test_ShortcutConfiguration`. Each of the four colour schemes is evaluated on its own \u2014 the slide as committed has both flags false, so only `light` would otherwise be exercised \u2014 and pinned pairwise-distinct, which is what catches a flag being ignored. `docs/legacy-{light,dark}-palette.txt` record the hand-written palettes the derivation replaced.\n\n## Issues (to be addressed in future prs)\n\n- There's a lot of duplication between configuration mode and scratchmode\n  - The original version had them combined and I split them up. I think we want them to be able to diverge more and need a better story for the common parts between editors.\n- Colour updates are applied by side effect \u2014 `JsUtil.set_css_variable` writes CSS custom properties straight onto `documentElement`. Threading them through the model would mean passing actions up/down the model stack. (Shortcuts do go through the model, via `Settings.Update.SetShortcutOverrides`.)\n- Stronger analyzed types for these editors \u2014 e.g. constraining a role to a colour-valued expression \u2014 are still future work.\n- **TODO: we need some way of enforcing that new colours stay themable.** Nothing currently stops a colour being written straight into a stylesheet, and that is how the un-themable ones accumulated \u2014 `make lint-css` checks that roles are bare aliases and ratchets dangling refs, but never asks whether a colour a stylesheet paints is reachable from the theme at all.\n\n",
